1
00:00:04.076 --> 00:00:04.000
Now that we learned about collaboration, let's talk about communication.

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.640
And what I mean by that is there is no one-size-fits-all for multi-agent systems.

3
00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:13.600
As any technical system out there, it takes you to really think about the architecture.

4
00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:16.319
This is going to be very exciting, so let's jump right into that.

5
00:00:16.879 --> 00:00:22.159
So you already have seen how much you can build with a single agent or even with multi-agents.

6
00:00:22.159 --> 00:00:26.639
And we talked about how much control you have when you're trying to set all the different

7
00:00:26.639 --> 00:00:30.479
aspects of these agents and even what LLMs they're going to be using.

8
00:00:30.479 --> 00:00:34.880
But one challenge with the multi-agent systems is that you have to define on how they will

9
00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:38.720
collaborate with each other. And that can really get interesting.

10
00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:42.959
Now, if you look at the default behavior in CrewAI, it's that the agents will work in a

11
00:00:42.959 --> 00:00:47.599
sequentially manner. And that means that you define your agents, and the tasks will define

12
00:00:47.599 --> 00:00:52.720
what orders things are going to be processed. A good example here might be stock trading

13
00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:57.439
analysis, where you have an initial agent picking up a stock, a second agent that is

14
00:00:57.439 --> 00:01:01.759
then given that pick is doing a deep research and extracting a bunch of information, and

15
00:01:01.759 --> 00:01:04.559
then a final agent that is putting a report together.

16
00:01:04.559 --> 00:01:09.599
So in this case, one task's output feeds into the next task that is going on.

17
00:01:09.599 --> 00:01:14.160
So in addition to this sequential flow, you can also enable these agents to delegate work

18
00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:17.040
among themselves and ask questions to each other.

19
00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:22.239
So if at any point in time, for example, the last reporting agent has a question about

20
00:01:22.239 --> 00:01:27.599
why was this ticker actually picked, you can ask back to the initial agent why there

21
00:01:27.599 --> 00:01:33.680
was a reason around that. Now, the idea here is that throughout this execution, each agent's

22
00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:38.800
task served as context for the next one. So it means that it's going to be injected

23
00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:43.919
into the prompt that goes into the next agent. Now, sometimes you want to have a little more

24
00:01:43.919 --> 00:01:48.400
control than that, and you want to make sure that you're feeding one task and one agent

25
00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:53.919
with the outputs of many different tasks. And here would be a good example for that,

26
00:01:53.919 --> 00:01:58.720
a coding crew. So these are agents that are meant to be creating software.

27
00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:04.239
So you might have an initial planner agent and a tester agent and then a coder agent.

28
00:02:04.239 --> 00:02:09.600
So the planner will think about the spec and how it can actually achieve that final goal.

29
00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:13.919
And the tester agent would then take that plan and use that to write some series of

30
00:02:13.919 --> 00:02:18.720
unit tasks. But the coder is the agent that is actually getting a lot of this work done,

31
00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:24.080
but it needs access to both the plan itself and the tasks.

32
00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:29.039
And the cool thing about this is that this is extremely easy to do in CrewAI.

33
00:02:29.039 --> 00:02:34.639
As you can see, if you have a plan task and you have a testing task that also has its own

34
00:02:34.639 --> 00:02:39.919
description and expected output, you can see that for the testing task, we're being a little more

35
00:02:39.919 --> 00:02:47.199
verbose than necessary and just indicating that the context for this task is the output of the

36
00:02:47.199 --> 00:02:54.720
plan task. And that is the default behavior in CrewAI. But then you can also get this final

37
00:02:54.720 --> 00:03:00.639
task that is creating the app. And this final task is now bringing together the results of

38
00:03:00.639 --> 00:03:05.039
both of those tasks, both the plan and the testing. And you can pinpoint that

39
00:03:05.039 --> 00:03:11.440
but that final context argument in there. Now, sequential is just the tip of the iceberg.

40
00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:16.639
There's so many more other processes. Parallel processing is another option in here.

41
00:03:16.639 --> 00:03:20.240
Of course, you don't need to stick with sequential for all the use cases.

42
00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:25.759
So in parallel, you might have many agents that are working at the same time doing their own task.

43
00:03:25.759 --> 00:03:30.160
So they might pass test results among themselves as they progress through.

44
00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:34.240
But the idea here is that they have the ability to get work done in parallel.

45
00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:39.520
So it reduces the latency drastically and how fast you can get your final output.

46
00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:45.440
You can also use a hierarchical process where you have one single agent that is delegating work to

47
00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:51.440
others. And you have this kind of manager agent that will not only delegate work, but it will

48
00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:57.839
also review the work once it's done. So this first agent is basically the boss of this other agent

49
00:03:57.839 --> 00:04:03.039
and is deciding who does what and then checks the output to make sure that it's up to par

50
00:04:03.039 --> 00:04:08.399
and ask them to do better in case it's not. This strategy can increase the processing time

51
00:04:08.399 --> 00:04:14.960
somewhat because you have extra LLM calls. But despite of the back and forth communication by

52
00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:19.359
design, it's very useful if you're trying to get something a little more tricky done.

53
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:25.839
In the end of the day, independently of the process that you choose, either sequential or

54
00:04:25.839 --> 00:04:31.920
parallel or hierarchical, there's always a memory layer that is present in every crew.

55
00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:36.320
And this memory layer is composed of the three memory types that we already talked on the

56
00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:41.920
previous module, short term, long term, and the entity memory. And I want to spend some time

57
00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:46.480
talking about how they impact the agent communication themselves. The short term

58
00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:52.559
memory is very ephemeral, meaning that it's cleared after every crew execution. The idea

59
00:04:52.559 --> 00:04:58.399
here is that all agents in the crew contribute to and share the same short memory storage.

60
00:04:58.399 --> 00:05:04.160
So the agents automatically access this short memory as part of CrewAI's context management,

61
00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:08.239
so they know what other agents have been working on and if there's any relevant

62
00:05:08.239 --> 00:05:13.600
information that they could use from there. Just keep in mind that during parallel execution,

63
00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:18.959
any given agent might not be done with their tasks at any given time, so its output might

64
00:05:18.959 --> 00:05:23.920
not be on the shared short term memory yet. Now, if you think about the long term memory,

65
00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:30.320
this is also shared, but this is more persistent. Agents will use this memory across many different

66
00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:36.640
executions. So these agents will self-critique as they go and as they finish their tasks and

67
00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:42.480
learn from each other's mistakes. So if one agent makes a mistake, the other agents can also use

68
00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:49.040
and learn from by tapping into this long term memory. And entity memory works similarly, where

69
00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:55.119
crews don't have to learn about the same objects, people, or places over and over again, because as

70
00:05:55.119 --> 00:06:01.760
they do, they write into that entity memory that they can all tap into. So if one agent learns

71
00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:07.760
about a particular person that it has done research on, for example, other agents can see that and

72
00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:13.119
won't ask it to research that person again if they can just retrieve it from the entity memory.

73
00:06:13.679 --> 00:06:20.160
I do want to highlight that among the communication patterns, there are some common coordination issues

74
00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:25.040
that you should be watching for when building your own use cases. And there is basically a couple that

75
00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:32.480
we see quite a lot. One is doing redundant work, where agents overlap and perform the same tasks

76
00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:38.239
or very similar tasks using the same tools and running the same searches, leading to a lot of

77
00:06:38.239 --> 00:06:44.640
inefficiency. So if the agents are stepping on each other's toes, you should tighten the scope of each

78
00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:50.640
task so they don't overlap. And you can also try starting with a parallel process and then moving

79
00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:56.079
into complexity to a hierarchical when needed. I definitely have seen the sequential process get

80
00:06:56.079 --> 00:07:02.880
most of the work done. Another issue arises when you have a very slow series of chains of these

81
00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:09.679
high latency step-by-step decision makings. This often happens when you add more agents and tasks

82
00:07:09.679 --> 00:07:15.440
without distinguishing what can run concurrently versus what can run sequentially, which slows down

83
00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:21.119
the whole automation process. And that's because you're making your agents depend on tasks that are

84
00:07:21.119 --> 00:07:26.399
happening earlier when they might not even necessarily need the results of those tasks to do

85
00:07:26.399 --> 00:07:32.000
their jobs. So to avoid this, make sure that you identify the tasks that can run asynchronously

86
00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:37.359
and mark them with the async_execution equals True. And that will make sure that all those

87
00:07:37.359 --> 00:07:42.799
executions are done in parallel. And if you want to refer back to those tasks outputs, you can then

88
00:07:42.799 --> 00:07:48.799
use the context argument, as we saw early, to basically bring that context into the different

89
00:07:48.799 --> 00:07:56.079
tasks. Now let's zoom out for a second. You really have so many options. We're talking about sequential,

90
00:07:56.079 --> 00:08:00.799
we're talking about hierarchical, you're talking about more of a hybrid approach where one agent

91
00:08:00.799 --> 00:08:05.600
is sending tasks to many agents, and then the context is bringing that back into one single

92
00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:10.720
agent. We're talking about asynchronous execution by doing these things in parallel. There's just

93
00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:16.000
a lot of things going on in there. So there are really so many different ways you can piece

94
00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:20.799
together these systems and allows you to define not only how your agents work together, but how

95
00:08:20.799 --> 00:08:25.839
much information they exchange with each other. So they have a really granular control over the

96
00:08:25.839 --> 00:08:31.519
context that each agent has for completing its own task. In the next one, I'm going to have

97
00:08:31.519 --> 00:08:36.559
something very exciting to share, because we're going to implement some of these new coordination

98
00:08:36.559 --> 00:08:42.080
patterns using the CrewAI CLI, which is one of my favorite tools, by the way. So I'm extremely

99
00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:45.520
excited about that one, and I want to see you in there right now.
